// ADR 0015 D11 / filed-defect register D47: the UNBOUND form of the heap-indirected
// `unwrap_or`, and the shape the refusal actually exists to eliminate.
//
// ⚠ THE TWO STOP MODES WERE DIFFERENT, AND ONLY THIS ONE BROKE A PROJECT RULE.
// Measured before the gate landed:
//   * bound   — `let g: P = unwrap_or(o, d); g.v` reached inkwell's `verify()` and
//     surfaced a diagnostic ("Invalid operands for select instruction!");
//   * unbound — `unwrap_or(o, d).v` PANICKED first, at
//     `inkwell-0.5.0/src/values/enums.rs:333` ("Found PointerValue ... but expected the
//     StructValue variant"), before verify ever ran.
// A panic on user-program input is exactly what `docs/project-context.md` forbids
// ("Don't `unwrap()` / `panic!` on user-program input — surface a `miette` diagnostic"),
// and it fired on a program the type checker ACCEPTED.
//
// ⚠ ITS SIBLINGS PIN THE PAYLOAD AXIS; THIS FILE PINS THE POSITION AXIS.
// `c15_unwrap_or_heap_payload` and `c17_unwrap_or_heap_payload_generic` both use the
// BOUND form and differ in payload kind (`?Struct` vs `?GenericInstance`), so between
// them they pin the gate's one match arm. Neither reaches the unbound position — so
// without this file the panicking shape, the headline of the change, would have been
// covered by nothing at all. A later refactor that moved the check somewhere only the
// bound form flows through would leave both siblings green and put the panic back.
//
// Same struct, same payload, same diagnostic — only the position of the call differs.
struct P { v: i64 }

fn main() -> i64 {
    let o: ?P = null;
    let d: P = P { v: 42 };
    unwrap_or(o, d).v
}
